	\section{Two-Register/Move Operations}
	This command was used extensively in the previous two sections, so little additional context is needed. This section simply addresses the ability to set condition flags and move information \emph{between} registers.
	
	\begin{lstlisting}
	MOV r0, #0x6666			@ Put bit pattern 011001100110 into BOTTOM two bytes of r0
	MVT r0, #0x6666			@ Put bit pattern 011001100110 into TOP two bytes of r0
	MOVS r1, r2				@ Put #0x0 from register two into register one, setting zero bit
	MVN r3, #0				@ Fill r3 with ones (Unsets, then inverts all bits in register)
	MVNS r1, #0				@ Fill r1 with ones and set negative bit
	\end{lstlisting}
	\section{Flexible Second Operand}
	
	This section introduces the concept of inline bit-shifting operations. This technique allows two mathematical or logical operations (from a limited subset) to be performed in the same clock cycle. To demonstrate this, various operations were performed with both the "modify" and "not-modify" versions of common commands
	
	\begin{lstlisting}
	MOV r0, #3					@ Put 3 into register 0
	MOV r1, #3					@ Put 3 into register 1

	ADD r2, r1, r0, LSL #3		@ Add 3 and 3, shift result 3 bits leftward

	ADDS r3, r1, r0, LSL #30	@ Move a 1 to MSb location, setting N flag
	
	SUB r4, r3, r2, ASR #4 		@ Subtract large number from small number, arithmetic shift right 3 positions@ 1 remains in MSb, N bit not set

	SUBS r5, r3, r2, ASR #4 	@ Subtract large number from small number, arithmetic shift right 3 positions@ 1 remains in MSb, N and NOT CARRY bits set
	
	ORR r6, r3, r4, ROR #2		@ Finds all locations where r3 or r4 have a bit, roll result 2 locations Right
	ORRS r7, r3, r4, ROR #2		@ Finds all locations where r3 or r4 have a bit, roll result 2 locations Right, sets negative bit
	\end{lstlisting}

	
	\section{Flag Setting/Comparison Operations}
	
	This class of operation allows the conditional information from a logical or mathematical operation to be encoded in the \texttt{cpsr} register, when the actual result is not needed.

	\begin{lstlisting}
	MOV r0, #3				@ Put 3 into register 0
	MOV r1, #3				@ Put 3 into register 1

	CMP r0, r1				@ Computes 3-3, sets Z flag and 'NOT BORROW' flag, as op requires no borrow
	CMP r0, #4				@ Computes 3-4 sets N flag, unsets C flag

	TST r0, #3				@ Computes 3 AND 3, clears Z flag as result is true
	
	TEQ r0, #3				@ Computes 3 XOR 3, sets Z flag due to equivalence
	\end{lstlisting}
	
	\section{Memory Addressing Formats}

	Addressed memory is a fundamental feature of modern digital computers. The ARM architecture has multiple methods of addressing memory for read- and write operations, covering a variety of different contexts.
	
	\subsection{Register Offset}
	In register offset addressing, the value stored in a register is used to offset the memory location encoded in the second operand. To demonstrate this functionality, a value was stored in the 32-bit memory location beginning at $0x00000+4$, then read to another register.
	
	\begin{lstlisting}
	MOV r0, #0			@ Base Location
	MOV r1, #4			@ For jump
	MOV r2, #0x00001234		@ Information to store

	STR r2, [r0, r1]	@ STORE r2 in r0+r1 memory location
	LDR r3, [r0, r1]	@ READ previously written data to r3
	\end{lstlisting}
	
	\subsection{Immediate Offset}
	In imediate offset addressing, the memory address stored in a register is offset by an immediate constant. To demonstrate this functionality, a value was stored in the 32-bit memory location beginning at $0x000000 + 8$, then read to another register.
	
	\begin{lstlisting}
	MOV r0, #0			@ Base Location
	MOV r1, #0x00001234	@ Data to be written
                
	STR r1, [r0, #8]	@ Store data from r1 in r0+8 memory location
	LDR r2, [r0, #8]	@ Read previously written data in r0+8, store in r2
	\end{lstlisting}

	\subsection{Writeback Addressing}
	
	This addressing syntax updates a memory address in a register by a specific offset, and accesses the memory at the updated value.
	
	\begin{lstlisting}
	MOV r1, #0x00001234 @ Information to store

	STR r1, [r0, #8]!	@ STORE r1 to r0+8 memory location. Update r0 to r0+8
	LDR r2, [r0]		@ READ previously written data, store in r2
	\end{lstlisting}
	
	\subsection{Post-Indexed Addressing}
	
	This addressing syntax accesses memory at an address specified by a value stored in a register ($rx[k]$), then updates that value by a specified offset: $rx[k+1]=rx[k]+C$.
	
	\begin{lstlisting}
	MOV r0, #0
	MOV r1, #0x5678		@ Information to store in bottom 2 memory bytes
	MOVT r1 #0x1234		@ Information to store in top 2 memory bytes
	MOV r3, #0xBEEF		@ Data to overwrite in final operation
	
	STR r0, [r0], #8	@ STORE r0 at r0[k] addressed memory. r0[k+1] = r0[k] + 8
	STR r1, [r0]		@ STORE r1 to r0[k+1] addressed memory
	LDR r2, [r0]		@ READ previously written data (r1), store in r2
	LDR r3, [#0]		@ r0[k]'th memory location has #0x0 stored
	\end{lstlisting}
	
	\subsection{Scaled Register Offset}
	
	This addressing syntax accesses memory at an address specified by a value stored in a register, with an offset scaled by $2^{n}$. The base address is not updated in this instance, except where modified by $!$.
	
	\begin{lstlisting}	
	MOV r1, #1				@ Offset to be scaled
	
	MOV r2, #0x5678			@ Information to store in bottom 2 memory bytes
	MOVT r2 #0x1234			@ Information to store in top 2 memory bytes
	
	@ Store information from r2 to memory location 0x0 + r1*2^2=0x4:
	STR r2, [r0, r1, LSL #2] 
	
	@ Using immediate offset addressing to confirm that the data was written to the correct address
	LDR r3, [r0, #4]
	
	@ Zero-Out r3
	MOV r3, #0
	
	@ Using Scaled Register Offset addressing to confirm that syntax works for both load and store operations, while also demonstrating writeback functionality
	LDR r3, [r0, r1, LSL #2]!
	
	@ Demonstrate that address register was updated
	LDR r4, [r0]
	\end{lstlisting}
